#Problem APEX 1.4.12

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Limits and continuity)
## DBsection(Finding limits using graphs)
## KEYWORDS('limit','one-sided', 'graph')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('1.4')
## Problem1('12')
## Updated July 2023

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Numeric")->variables->add(a => 'Real');

$a = random(1, 3);

$plot = Plot(rounded_corners => 1);
for my $i (-4 .. 3) {
    $plot->add_dataset(
        [ $i, $i ], [ $i + 1, $i ],
        start_mark => 'circle',
        end_mark   => 'open_circle',
        color      => 'blue',
        width      => 1
    );
}

$alttext =
    'A graph of f(x), which consists of horizontal line segments.  '
    . 'On [-4,3) the value is -4, on [-3, 2) the value is -3 and so on until '
    . 'on [3,4), the value is 3.';

$ans1 = Compute("$a-1")->reduce;
$ans2 = Compute("$a");
$ans3 = Compute("DNE");
$ans4 = Compute("-$a-1")->reduce;
$ans5 = Compute("-$a");
$ans6 = Compute("DNE");
$ans7 = "a - 1";
$ans8 = "a";
$ans9 = Compute("DNE");

BEGIN_PGML

Evaluate each expression using the given graph of [`f(x)`].

Enter *DNE* if the limit or value does not exist.

If you are having a hard time seeing the picture clearly, click on the picture. It will expand to a larger picture on its own page so that you can inspect it more clearly.

>> [![$alttext]!]{$plot}{400} <<

---
a) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to [$a]^{-}}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans1}{20}

b) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to [$a]^{+}}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans2}{20}

c) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to [$a]}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans3}{20}

d) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -[$a]^{-}}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans4}{20}

e) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -[$a]^{+}}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans5}{20}

f)  [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -[$a]}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans6}{20}

    In problems g-i, assume [`a`] is an *integer* such that 
[`-3 \leq a \leq 3`].

g) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a^{-}}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans7}{20}

h) [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a^{+}}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans8}{20}

i)  [`\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a}f(x) = `] [_]{$ans9}{20}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) This is the one-sided limit on the right as [` x\to [$a]`]. Looking only at the values less than [$a] shows that the limit is [$ans1]. 

b) This is the one-sided limit on the left as [` x\to [$a]`]. Looking only at the values greather than [$a] shows that the limit is [$ans2]. 

c) Since the two one-sided limits are not equal, the limit does not exist. 

d) This is the one-sided limit on the right as [` x\to -[$a]`]. Looking only at the values less than [`[$a*-1]`] shows that the limit is [$ans4]. 

e) This is the one-sided limit on the left as [` x\to -[$a]`]. Looking only at the values greather than [`[$a*-1]`] shows that the limit is [$ans5]. 

f) Since the two one-sided limits are not equal, the limit does not exist. 

g) Again, this is similar to a) and d) except for a general value [`a`].  The limit is [`[$ans7]`].

h) Again, this is similar to b) and e) except for a general value [`a`].  The limit is [`[$ans8]`].

i) Again, similar to c) and f), this limit does not exist. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
